[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was merged on subject lines ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
and marked known as one bug: the parser reads dates month first. Reading every report body next to the
tenant directory shows **three problems** inside it. Each has its own ticket now, with the reports it covers,
in [AdamaOuedraogo/support-kb-4471](https://github.com/AdamaOuedraogo/support-kb-4471/issues).

<Callout kind="warn">
One fix is queued for #4471. It clears the first problem only. The other two stay open until they get their own fix.
</Callout>

## The split

Every report is counted once. The export has overlapping pages, so rows are deduplicated by id first; the
table below is built from `reports-4471.json`, the tenant files and `desk-notes.json` at render time.

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="AdamaOuedraogo/support-kb-4471" tickets={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, 'one-day-move': 3 }} />

## 1 · `parser` — the shared parser reads month first

Every tenant on file writes the day first (checked against the platform's locale data, not a typed list).
The shared parser reads `MM/DD`, so a day of 1 to 12 swaps with the month: a booking for 7 April goes out
as 4 July. Days above 12 cannot be read month first, which is why no report quotes one.

This is every report that came in through the app or by email, plus form reports from `booking-v2`
tenants, whose form prints the right `DD/MM/YYYY` hint. It is what #4471 was meant to be, and the queued
fix (parse with the tenant's locale) covers it.

## 2 · `hint` — booking-v3 tells day-first tenants to type month first

The `booking-v3` form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. Three day-first tenants run it
(alder.physio, copper.studio, pale.harbour). Their customers type what the hint says, month first, and the
form reads it the tenant's way, day first. The symptom is the same month flip, so the subject lines match
and the desk merged them, but the cause is in the form, not the parser. Fixing the parser leaves this
in place; the hint must follow the tenant's locale, or the field must be read in the order the hint shows.

## 3 · `one-day-move` — the date shifts by one day

A few reports describe no month flip at all. The date moves by exactly one day, the same meeting lands
on different days in Lisbon and Toronto, or an invite is stamped before the booking was made. That is a
date-boundary problem: the booking's date crosses a time-zone offset or the UTC day line between booking
and invite. The desk had read these as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`) but left them in #4471 because
their subjects matched.

<OneDayMoves />

## Why one ticket swallowed three problems

- **Dedupe by subject** ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)) merges on the subject line
  alone. Twelve subject lines cover all three problems, so matching subjects said "same issue" every time.
- **Intake's tag is a guess** from the reporter's browser locale. A tenant in Germany whose staff run an
  `en-US` browser was tagged `hint`; a `booking-v3` form report from a Portuguese browser was tagged
  `parser`. The table above shows how often the tag and the cause disagree. The cause depends on the
  tenant and the channel, which intake never looks up.
- **The page was written from the queue, not from the data.** The incident page typed "one issue" into a
  paragraph while its own table, built from the data, already showed two causes and a footnote.

## What the desk should do

1. Keep #4471 as the `parser` ticket, with its count corrected to the first row above.
2. Track the `hint` and `one-day-move` problems on their own tickets (linked in the table).
3. Change triage so a merge checks the tenant's locale and form, not the subject alone. The
   [dedupe explorer](/kb/triage/dedupe) already runs that check; make it the rule.

